messages:
Maya: Heads-up: Need to lock PRD acceptance criteria for the tool-calling / structured-output fallback behavior by EOD. Link: https://redwood/doc/prd/tool-fallbacks (draft). Main decision: opt-in strict validation vs. permissive with normalization. Thoughts?
Jon: IMO go permissive by default with explicit strict mode. Customers want fewer 500s. Edge cases to cover: missing fields, extra unknown keys, type coercion (int vs string), enum mismatches, nested arrays.
Priya: Customer ask from Acme: wants deterministic fallback to text-only when schema mismatch, plus audit log of normalization. For launch we should support both `drop-unknown` and `coerce-types` flags.
Liam: Operational checklist: SLOs for per-request validation latency <10ms, fallback success rate >= 99.5%, and metrics emitted: fallback_type, validation_latency_ms, original_payload_size. Need canary rollout plan + automatic rollback if error-rate spike > 1%.
Eva: UX notes: when fallback happens show structured error plus human-friendly hint. For function-calls, return: {status:'fallback', reason:'coerce_types', example:'...'} . Also ensure we don't leak raw user data in logs.
Jon: Prototype schema snippet (for discussion): ```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "user_id": {
      "type": "string"
    },
    "items": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "id": { "type": "string" }
        }
      }
    }
  },
  "additionalProperties": false
}
``` With fallback we could set additionalProperties:true but strip unknown keys.
Maya: Draft acceptance checklist (short): 1) Validation passes for canonical tests (1000 prompt variations), 2) Fallback preserves intent for >95% eval, 3) Telemetry shows fallback <5% on our test tenants, 4) No PII in plain logs, 5) Docs & SDK examples updated. Any missing?
Priya: Add: Rollout gating: require sign-off from security for logging, and export a 'rollback if churn' pass.
Liam: I'll add monitoring dashboards and alert runbook. Can we add a small canary (5% of traffic) for 48h, then 25% for 24h, then full? @Maya
Maya: Yes, canary plan accepted. I'll update PRD and ping eng for RFC review tomorrow morning. Thanks :thumbsup:
